                            <a name="comment3138"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
"Mr. Prager contributed one 5,000-word story in December 2006 that was his total contribution to the paper for the year. "<br />
<br />
Is that a staff job?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:alrikabi@gmail.com">ralrika</a>
	

 at January 16,2008 10:18
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3139"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
But at a meeting on Jan. 10 at Bayard&#8217;s, new publisher Robert Thomson joked that a leder shouldn&#8217;t take as long to turn around as the &#8220;gestation of a llama,&#8221; or about 350 days.<br />
<br />
Makes sense to me. A 5,000-word piece might take 350 days to do, or more, whatever it takes, but that doesn't mean you can't be doing some other things within that span of time.<br />
<br />
Right?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

 at January 16,2008 12:10
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3141"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
"A 5,000-word piece might take 350 days to do, or more, whatever it takes, but that doesn't mean you can't be doing some other things within that span of time.<br />
<br />
Right?"<br />
<br />
Perhaps. But the broader point that Murdoch thinks the Journal's A1 stories - for my money some of the best journalism being done on a daily basis - are too long for the most sophisticated readership in newspapers? Well, that's pretty disheartening. 
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:tlogan@post-dispatch">Tim</a>
	

 at January 16,2008 12:28
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3143"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
OK, who amongst all of us has time to read the WSJ's long A1 feature every day on a weekday? <br />
<br />
And, no, setting it aside for the weekend doesn't count. <br />
<br />
And be honest about your answer ...
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:tbond@hotmail.com">Tom Bond</a>
	

 at January 16,2008 17:33
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3144"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
No one has the time to read everything in the newspaper. And no one ever has.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

 at January 16,2008 19:08
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3145"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
There's something important here, as all sorts of newspapers consider slimming down or whatever. <br />
<br />
Tom: I don't read them very often. When I do, though, I do full on. And I'm always glad I did. So what does that mean?<br />
<br />
I've subscribed to the New Yorker going on four years. I'm sometimes embarassed at how much exists in each magazine that I don't actually read.<br />
<br />
The thing is, I'm so happy they exist. I pay for them to exist. I'd pay more.<br />
<br />
If just 10 percent of readers make it past the jump, as someone suggested in an earlier post, should papers dictate a mandatory length limit? Should we reduce the size of the paper because folks can't finish it?<br />
<br />
In my view, it's all about who is writing. And if it's someone good, if it's a good story, it feels like a treat, it feels like I got a deal on the morning paper. Isn't that what we're looking for? <br />
<br />
PS: I wonder how much J-time has been wasted in the past few days reading about/talking about/blogging about 'The Wire.' I also wonder if we'd all be telling better stories if the internet didn't keep us strapped to the desk. 
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:bmontgomery@sptimes.com">Ben</a>
	

 at January 16,2008 20:55
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3146"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
This whole "having no time to read" explanation continues to puzzle me. <br />
<br />
Why is there less time now than before?<br />
<br />
Is it work? But my parents have worked 50 hours a week for about 30 years now, while raising three kids, yet they manage to read the NY Times every day.<br />
<br />
More things to do? Maybe, but how many people do you know do 10 different things every week? Most only keep it to a few things.<br />
<br />
What makes our lives so complicated that we can't make time to read something?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:scriptorludi@yahoo.com">Keith</a>
	

 at January 17,2008 07:26
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3147"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
Keith:<br />
<br />
It's because the media constantly TELL us we're too busy. <br />
<br />
Newspapers brought much of the current predicament on themselves. If we had been giving people really interestingreads all these years, they wouldn't have deserted us. Readers would have given us more of their time as they did in years past.<br />
<br />
The other sea change has been a plethora of other diversions in the home. When I was a kid, there were 20 or so cable channels, radionand newspapers. Now there are Wiis, Playstations, 200-plus cable channels, the Internet, satellite radion, movies on demand, Netflix, etc. and -- most importantly -- two working adults in most households. <br />
<br />
But you have a good point: Given that almost all meals are cooked outside the home (among other demographic trends), are we REALLY busier than we were 10, 20 or 30 years ago?<br />
<br />

</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		Tom Bond	

 at January 17,2008 08:31
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3148"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
Perhaps this is a clue, as well: In researching whether there was a baby boom after our last big blizzard (there wasn't) I discovered that apparently baby booms only occur after blizzards if the power goes out. Seems that all-things electric are even better than sex. If newspapers aren't filled with stuff like I see on Gangrey all the time, then where do you think they fall on that priority list?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:dstockman@jg.net">Dan Stockman</a>
	

 at January 17,2008 09:33
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3149"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
Way I see it, anything worth doing takes time, right? And if you've got to take time, you've got to make time, right? So it's our job to make folks make time. That's ALWAYS been our job.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

 at January 17,2008 09:43
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3150"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
Also: I don't think this conversation should be about length of stories. A story can be 500 words, 2,000 words, 5,000 words, whatever -- if they're in, they're in, they'll keep going, and if it's no good, they aren't going to read it. And shouldn't.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

 at January 17,2008 09:51
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3151"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
I agree with that. There are some 1,200 word stories that I cannot finish. There are 7,000 word stories that I wish would go on forever. There doesn't need to be a limit on story length. There needs to be a limit on boring stories. <br />
<br />
I think that part of the problem -- I don't want to open up a can of worms here -- but how many editors really know what makes a story compelling as entertainment not just as journalism? Because you need both for someone to read that much. It has to inform, but it also has to have serious dramatic pull through. <br />
<br />
Thoughts?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:wrightespn@gmail.com">Wright</a>
	

 at January 17,2008 12:29
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3157"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Burying The 'Leder'
</h4>

<p>
Don't blame the editors quite so much. Most editors I've worked with know a good tale and will fight for it. What really hacks some editors off is pointless "narrative" that really isn't (and there's a lot of that in the mass media), the mentaility that length equals substance and quality (it doesn't) and the primadonna idea that "I have to be allowed to write as long as I want to because this is a breathless narrative." <br />
<br />
Let's face it: Some ultra-long, 5,ooo-word pieces are largely done for the ego of the writer. Some of what I see at that length is just journalistic onanism meant to appeal to collegaues in the media and academics. 
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		Tom Bond	

 at January 18,2008 09:22
</div>
